└── Modbus TCP client for reading/writing holding registers to industrial field devices
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk, minimalmodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient(host, port=port), client.connect(), client.read_holding_registers(address, 1, unit=self.unit), rr.isError(), 
    │   rr.registers[0], client.write_register(address, counts, unit=self.unit), wr.isError()
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus wraps responses in objects with .isError() and .registers, while pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk typically return plain values or None 
    │   on failure, requiring a different error-handling model; minimalmodbus's primary API targets serial/RTU communication; its TCP support and function 
    │   signatures differ significantly (no 'unit' keyword, different read/write register method names), requiring a connector/adapter layer; pymodbus uses a 
    │   'unit' kwarg for slave/unit id; other libraries use 'slave_id' or positional arguments; Connection lifecycle methods differ: connect()/close() 
    │   semantics vary slightly between libraries; Register addressing and function-code selection conventions can differ across libraries (e.g., holding vs 
    │   input registers)
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All four packages provide Modbus TCP master/client functionality capable of reading and writing holding registers, which is the only operation this 
        file performs. pymodbus is a full-featured async/sync Modbus stack; pyModbusTCP is a lightweight TCP-only client with a similar read/write register 
        API; modbus-tk supports Modbus TCP master mode with comparable register read/write calls; minimalmodbus is primarily serial/RTU-focused but does 
        support TCP mode and exposes read/write register functions, making it a plausible (though more divergent) substitute as explicitly called out in the 
        file's own docstring.

